From: Mdjavad2nd prf <md.javad01@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 00:27:56 +0330
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] feat(playback): expiring signed URLs via a PHP gateway
 (v1.8.0, Apache)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Release blocker. POST /lessons/{id}/playback returned a PERMANENT url
(https://dll.mrdastbaaf.com/FA/lesson-01.mp4). Anyone who captured it once —
from the Network tab, a proxy log, a screenshot — could download the file
forever, with no login. The only protection was Referer-based hotlink
blocking, defeated by a forged header.

Playback URLs are now short-lived and signed:

  https://dll.mrdastbaaf.com/s.php?f=%2FFA%2Flesson-01.mp4&e=1800000000&u=42&t=<hmac>
                                    path        expiry          userId     signature

s.php verifies the signature, resolves the path safely, and streams the file
with full Range support.

This replaces an earlier draft of this change that relied on nginx's
secure_link module. The download host is Apache 2.4.67, so the verification
moved into PHP. That is not a cosmetic difference: nginx would have served
the bytes statically, whereas the gateway reads and writes them itself, so
Range, Content-Length, Content-Type and output buffering all become our
responsibility. The gateway does all four explicitly.

Signature:
  message   = "<percent-DECODED path>|<expiry>|<userId>"
  signature = hash_hmac('sha256', message, secret)     // hex, 64 chars

Design decisions worth reviewing:

- ONE implementation, two consumers. gateway/signed-url-core.php holds the
  pure algorithm and is included by BOTH the plugin and the gateway. The
  plugin's service class now does no cryptography of its own. Two independent
  implementations would eventually drift, and the symptom would be every
  stream 403ing in production. A test pins the two sides equal.

- The signature is over the DECODED path while the URL carries the encoded
  one. Every filename in this project is Persian, so signing the encoded form
  would invalidate every signature. Pinned by a test.

- Hex, not base64. base64 puts +, / and = into a query string, where each has
  a different meaning. The gateway also parses QUERY_STRING by hand instead of
  reading $_GET, because PHP turns '+' into a space in $_GET while
  rawurlencode emits %2B — so a filename containing '+' round-trips exactly.

- Path safety is four layers: null bytes rejected, any ".." segment rejected
  (after normalising backslashes), a closed extension allow-list, and a
  final realpath containment check that also catches symlinks. A valid
  signature is NOT authorisation to read any file: a correctly signed
  traversal still 404s.

- Rollout order is gateway first, signing flag second, block direct access
  LAST. If nginx-style enforcement goes in before PHP starts signing, every
  stream 403s. is_enabled() therefore requires BOTH the flag and a configured
  gateway URL, defaults to OFF, and maybe_sign() is a no-op when off — so the
  call site needs no guard and enabling without configuring cannot emit a
  broken URL.

- The secret comes from MRDASTBAAF_SIGNED_URL_SECRET in wp-config.php when
  defined, falling back to an option. Keeping it out of the options table
  keeps it out of database backups and out of alloptions on every request.

- Expiry is capped at 24h so a misconfiguration cannot quietly restore a
  permanent URL, and the gateway re-checks that ceiling independently.

- The response gains signedUrls (bool) so support can tell "no signature"
  from "bad signature" without shell access.

Scope: only /playback output is signed. Proxy mode is untouched and
proxyMode:true remains unsupported by the client by agreement.

Deliverables:
- gateway/s.php, gateway/SignedUrlGateway.php, gateway/signed-url-core.php
- gateway/signed-url-config.php.sample, gateway/htaccess.sample
- docs/APACHE_SIGNED_URLS.md — deployment, the four-step rollout, verification
  commands, rollback, and a 403 troubleshooting table
- docs/DLL_HOST_INSTALL.md and docs/DLL_HOST_HTACCESS.sample are marked
  superseded so nobody keeps maintaining the Referer approach

Tests: tests/php/test-signed-urls.php — 114 assertions: the fixed vector
cross-checked against an independent Python implementation, the Persian-path
vector, per-input sensitivity, encoded-vs-decoded divergence, the full
build->parse->verify round trip, traversal (including backslash and symlink),
the extension allow-list, every Range branch including 416, gateway status
codes, and the plugin/gateway drift guard.

Four assertions failed on first run and every one was my expectation, not the
code: base64 padding, parse_url on a non-URL, the expiry arithmetic, and one
test that used the test's secret instead of the plugin's generated one. Each
was verified against the runtime and the test corrected.

Suite total: 324 assertions across seven files.

Honest limits, also stated in the runbook: a user who is watching can still
capture the stream; the URL is reusable until it expires; and the gateway adds
PHP load per Range request (X-Sendfile is the upgrade path if that ever
becomes the bottleneck). Real segment-level protection needs HLS, which is not
built yet.
